+## 3. Findings
+
+Severity: High = a wrong or misleading number; Medium = hard to read or inconsistent; Low = polish.
+Screenshots are in `docs/screenshots/viz-review-beta-2/`.
+
+### V-36 (High) Group by Location: a city's row shows another city's posted range
+- View: Chart, Clusters, Group by Location, no filter. Repro: `#c=amazon&g=location`, the Nashville row;
+  `#c=microsoft&g=location`, Mountain View and New York.
+- Expected: a place's row uses the range posted for that place (as the chart already does under a Location
+  filter since SA-5, and as V-18 requires).
+- Actual: a posting with one range per place (`salary.ranges[].locations`: Amazon's pay rows, Microsoft's SB-5
+  SF Bay Area/NYC ranges) goes into the row of its first location, but is plotted at its headline range, which
+  is another place's. Amazon: 896 postings; "Nashville · 352 roles · median $129K", while Nashville's own posted
+  ranges give $116.4K (191 of its 339 postings are drawn at another city's range; e.g. amazon:10371179, drawn at
+  Bellevue's $82,700–117,100, Nashville's own row $60,200–105,400). Jersey City $180.7K drawn vs $198.7K own;
+  Boston $173.0K vs $169.1K. Microsoft: "Mountain View · 57 roles · median $209K" vs $246.1K own (39 of 57
+  drawn at the Redmond range), "New York · 18 roles · median $191K" vs $219.0K. Apple (merged requisitions):
+  Boulder $206.2K vs $192.1K.
+- Screenshots: `v-amazon-location-Nashville.png`, `v-microsoft-location-MountainView.png`,
+  `v-microsoft-location-NewYork.png`. Owner: `chart.js renderClusters()` (Location rows use `p.mid`, the
+  headline); `chartPay()` picks the place's own range only under a Location filter.
+
+### V-37 (High) V-18 is fixed in Clusters only: Ranges grouped by Location shows other countries' pay
+- View: Chart, Ranges, Group by Location. Repro: `#c=google&v=ranges&g=location`, the London group;
+  `#c=nvidia&v=ranges&g=location`, Munich.
+- Expected: as in Clusters (V-18), a place's group holds only pay posted for that place.
+- Actual: "London · 13 · median $253K": 5 of its bars are US-dollar ranges posted for Mountain View and New York
+  on DeepMind postings that list London first ("Research Scientist, Human-AI Interaction, DeepMind, $262K to
+  $364K"). "Zurich · 4 · median $84K" is euro ranges; NVIDIA "Munich · 1 · median $96K" is a Warsaw PLN range
+  (PLN 221,250–507,000). Clusters shows "no salary posted for here" for the same places.
+- Screenshots: `v-google-ranges-location-London.png`, `v-google-ranges-location-Zurich.png`,
+  `v-nvidia-ranges-location-Munich.png`. Owner: `chart.js groups()` (keys by `keyOf(job, 'location')`, no
+  `payCoversRow`).
+
+### V-35 (Medium) Striped bars that are one place's range, and the tooltip doesn't say which place
+- View: Chart, Ranges. Repro: `#c=amazon&v=ranges`; `#c=microsoft&v=ranges`, hover a striped "Senior Software
+  Engineer" bar.
+- Expected: the legend's "Striped: several levels or places" and the notes' "one posting's range over several
+  levels or places" describe every striped bar.
+- Actual: 2,201 Amazon bars and 236 Microsoft bars are striped but are one place's posted range
+  (`spanTiers().span === false`); 2,030 and 228 of them are the very range of a plain bar on the same board
+  (Microsoft IC4 $119,800–234,700 is drawn plain ×83 and striped ×12 in Software Engineering). The tooltip says
+  "The range for one place; the posting lists 4 places' ranges" and lists them, but not which one the bar is:
+  amazon:10371179 is drawn at Bellevue's $82.7K–117.1K, while its first location, the row it goes to under Group
+  by Location, is Nashville ($60.2K–105.4K). The legend's count, "Striped … 2,260", is 2,201 of these plus 59 real
+  spans.
+- Screenshot: `ranges-amazon-1440.png`. Owner: `chart.js buildRow()` (stripes any `p.span`), `showJobTip()`,
+  `renderLegend()`.
+
+### V-33 (Medium) Clusters: the circle plan never settles on Amazon and merges two pay peaks
+- View: Chart, Clusters, 1440 px. Repro: `#c=amazon`, row "Operations, IT, & Support Engineering".
+- Expected (chart.js comment): circles are drawn at the sizes they were planned with, and merge only when they
+  would touch.
+- Actual: the planning loop (`renderClusters`, the passes over `placeCircles`) alternates between a largest
+  circle of 1,123 and one of 1,665 and stops after pass 3 on the merged one. The row then has one circle "1,665
+  roles, midpoints $66K–$125K · median $93K", which holds two peaks, $87.4K (1,123 postings) and $107.7K (542),
+  with 48 postings between $95K and $105K. Planned at the drawn sizes (largest 1,665), the same rule keeps them
+  apart. The merged circle is wider than the old $50K bins. Replayed with the chart's own `placeCircles` on the
+  real midpoints (scratchpad `passes.mjs`); on a phone it settles at once.
+- Screenshot: `clusters-amazon-1440.png`. Owner: `chart.js renderClusters()` (the `for (let pass …)` loop).
+
+### V-34 (Medium) Clusters: most circles are drawn at the minimum size, so 1 and 123 postings look the same
+- View: Chart, Clusters, every board. Repro: `#c=amazon`, Software Development row.
+- Expected: a circle's size shows its count (the sqrt scale), or the circle says it.
+- Actual: radius = max(4.5, 16·√(n / largest)); with Amazon's largest circle at 1,665 every circle under 123
+  postings is the 9 px floor, with no count (counts need a 16 px circle). Software Development: one posted band
+  at $109.1K carried by 118 postings is the same dot as the one posting at $271K. Floor-size circles: Amazon 44
+  of 70 (up to 123 postings in one), Apple 47 of 60, Google 34 of 65, Meta 31 of 60, Microsoft 33 of 47, NVIDIA
+  20 of 39, Netflix 16 of 38, Anduril 45 of 51 (88%; 92% on a phone, up to 132 postings).
+- Screenshot: `clusters-amazon-1440.png`. Owner: `chart.js renderClusters()` (`radiusAt`, `rMin`).
+
+### V-38 (Medium) Ranges: a row of 775 postings looks like a row of one
+- View: Chart, Ranges. Repro: `#c=amazon&v=ranges`, the "×775" Software Development row ($143,700–194,400).
+- Expected: the drawing shows where postings concentrate (the user's "kinda all span too evenly").
+- Actual: after stacking, every distinct range is one 22 px row with the same bar; only the "×775" text differs.
+  Rows of one posting are 42–53% of the chart's height (Netflix 73%) but hold 7–16% of postings (Netflix 35%);
+  the busiest 10% of rows hold 53–74% of postings in 10% of the height. By $25K band the row share and the
+  posting share differ by 12–30% (total variation): Google's $250K–275K band is 12% of postings and 4% of the
+  rows, NVIDIA's $250K–275K 20% and 9%, Microsoft's $175K–200K 24% and 11%. The distribution strip above the
+  chart still shows postings.
+- Screenshots: `ranges-amazon-1440.png`, `ranges-google-1440.png`. Owner: `chart.js buildRow()` (no mark scales
+  with `p.n`).
